1. RESOLVED 2026-07-21 — DOES THE DEATH DOCTRINE APPLY TO ROBOT DEACTIVATION SPECIFICALLY?
Yes, fully and directly. Developer clarified robot canon: robots run on a "nano-architecture" gel brain (Ex
Machina "blue glowy" model), not a discrete/copyable data store — "backing up" or "uploading" a robot mind is
about as infeasible as uploading a human one. Irreparable damage to a robot's brain or energy source IS death
from the robot's own perspective, not a recoverable event. Robots are not immortal, just long-lived. This
means the death-as-changed-vantage doctrine applies to robot deactivation exactly the way it applies to
organic death, with no backup-mechanic tension to resolve. **Flag for broader project consistency: this
clarifies robot-consciousness canon beyond just this religion (Companion System, Calethina substrate-transfer
material, etc.) — worth cross-checking those areas for consistency with this clarification if not already
aligned.**

